   131	
   132	### Architecture Principles
   133	
   134	Diedrico follows strict architectural principles documented in [data-model.md](specs/001-dihedral-learning-mvp/data-model.md):
   135	
   136	1. **Parametric by default**: All geometric elements update when dependencies change
   137	2. **Explicit dependencies**: Process Tree tracks all element relationships
   138	3. **Command pattern**: All mutations through undoable commands
   139	4. **No implicit entities**: User must explicitly create all geometric objects
   140	5. **Plane-based organization**: 2D elements belong to specific projection planes
   141	6. **Type-safe domain model**: Comprehensive TypeScript types for all geometry
   142	
   143	### Key Concepts
   144	
   145	**Process Tree**: Every geometric element is created by an Action that tracks its inputs (dependencies). The Process Tree is the complete history of construction operations, enabling:
   146	- Parametric updates (change inputs, outputs recalculate)
   147	- Dependency visualization (see what depends on what)
   148	- Construction replay (understand the sequence)
   149	
   150	**Element Variations**: Each element type (Point, Line, Circle) has multiple construction methods. For example, points can be:
   151	- Free (positioned anywhere)
   152	- Intersection (where two elements cross)
   153	- On-element (constrained to a line or circle)
   154	- Transported (transferred between projection planes)
   155	
   156	**Realization**: The actual geometric representation (x, y coordinates, etc.) computed from an element's definition and dependencies. Realizations are cached and invalidated when dependencies change.
   157	
