===== docs/architecture/commands.md =====
     1	# Command System
     2	
     3	All mutations to the `World` go through the command system, enabling undo/redo, serialization, and history tracking.
     4	
     5	## Architecture
     6	
     7	```
     8	CommandStack
     9	├── undoStack: Command[]
    10	└── redoStack: Command[]
    11	
    12	Command
    13	├── operations: IOperation[]
    14	├── status: Building | Committed | Undone
    15	├── addAndExecute(op) → executes immediately, stores for undo
    16	├── commit() → finalizes, pushes to stack
    17	├── undo() → reverses all ops in reverse order
    18	└── redo() → reapplies all ops in original order
    19	```
    20	
    21	## IOperation Interface
    22	
    23	Every mutation is an `IOperation`:
    24	
    25	```typescript
    26	interface IOperation {
    27	  type: string
    28	  parameters: Record<string, unknown>
    29	  outputs: Record<string, unknown>
    30	  apply(world: World): OperationResult
    31	  undo(world: World): void
    32	  serialize?(): SerializedOperation
    33	}
    34	```
    35	
    36	**Requirements:**
    37	- **Deterministic** — same inputs always produce the same outputs
    38	- **Serializable** — can be saved to JSON and reconstructed
    39	- **Self-contained** — all data needed is in `parameters` and `outputs`
    40	- **Reversible** — `undo()` fully reverses `apply()`
    41	
    42	## Operation Categories
    43	
    44	### World Operations
    45	
    46	| Operation | Purpose |
    47	|-----------|---------|
    48	| `AddFreePoint2DOp` | Create a point at (u, v) on a plane |
    49	| `AddLine2DByTwoPointsOp` | Create line through two points |
    50	| `AddSegment2DByPointsOp` | Create segment between two points |
    51	| `AddCircle2DByCenterAndPointOp` | Create circle from center + radius point |
    52	| `AddGuide2DPerpendicularToLineOp` | Create guide perpendicular to hinge |
    53	| `AddSiblingGuide2DOp` | Auto-create paired guide on sibling plane |
    54	| `AddLineLineIntersectionPoint2DOp` | Intersection of two lines |
    55	| `AddLineCircleIntersectionPoint2DOp` | Line-circle intersection |
    56	| `AddCircleCircleIntersectionPoint2DOp` | Circle-circle intersection |
    57	| `AddSiblingIntersectionPoint2DOp` | Paired intersection on sibling plane |
    58	| `Add3DPointOp` | Create 3D point from projections |
    59	| `Add3DLineOp` | Create 3D line from projections |
    60	| `Add3DSegmentOp` | Create 3D segment from projections |
    61	| `Add3DPlaneOp` | Create 3D plane from three points |
    62	| `AddAuxiliaryProjectionPlaneOp` | Create new projection plane |
    63	| `AddBrokenHingeLineOp` | Add fold line for auxiliary plane |
    64	| `UpdateFreePoint2DPositionOp` | Move a free point |
    65	| `UpdateElementStyleOp` | Change visual style |
    66	| `RemovePoint2DOp` / `RemoveLine2DOp` / ... | Delete elements |
    67	
    68	### Process Tree Operations
    69	
    70	| Operation | Purpose |
    71	|-----------|---------|
    72	| `AddProcessTreeActionOp` | Record action in tree |
    73	| `AddProcessTreeActionGroupOp` | Create action group |
    74	| `PushSubprocessOp` / `PopSubprocessOp` | Manage subprocess stack |
    75	| `RenameSubprocessOp` | Rename subprocess |
    76	| `ToggleEncapsulationOp` | Toggle intermediate visibility |
    77	| `ToggleOutputOp` | Toggle output flag |
    78	
    79	## Command Lifecycle
    80	
    81	```
    82	1. Tool creates new Command
    83	2. Tool calls command.addAndExecute(op1)  → op1.apply(world)
    84	3. Tool calls command.addAndExecute(op2)  → op2.apply(world)
    85	4. If any op fails → entire command rolls back
    86	5. command.commit() → pushed to CommandStack.undoStack
    87	6. User presses Ctrl+Z → command.undo() → ops reversed
    88	7. User presses Ctrl+Y → command.redo() → ops reapplied
    89	```
    90	
    91	## Command Factories
    92	
    93	Factories encapsulate common multi-operation patterns:
    94	
    95	| Factory | Operations Created |
    96	|---------|-------------------|
    97	| `PointSequenceFactory` | Point + process tree entries |
    98	| `LineSequenceFactory` | Line + process tree entries |
    99	| `CircleSequenceFactory` | Circle + process tree entries |
   100	| `GuideSequenceFactory` | Guide + sibling guide + tree entries |
   101	| `IntersectionSequenceFactory` | Intersection points + siblings + tree entries |
   102	| `DeletionSequenceFactory` | Remove element + dependency cleanup |
   103	| `InitializationSequenceFactory` | World setup (planes, hinges, origin) |
   104	| `ProjectionSetupFactory` | Standard folding plane setup |
   105	
   106	## Serialization
   107	
   108	Commands serialize to JSON for persistence:
   109	
   110	```json
   111	{
   112	  "operations": [
   113	    {
   114	      "type": "AddFreePoint2DOp",
   115	      "parameters": { "planeId": "projPlane1", "u": 3.5, "v": 2.0 },
   116	      "outputs": { "pointId": "projPlane1/point2d5" }
   117	    }
   118	  ]
   119	}
   120	```
   121	
   122	The `ProjectService.reconstructOperation()` switch handles deserialization of all operation types.

===== docs/architecture/stores.md =====
     1	# Stores
     2	
     3	Diedrico uses Pinia for reactive state management. Each store handles a specific concern.
     4	
     5	## Store Overview
     6	
     7	| Store | Purpose |
     8	|-------|---------|
     9	| `processStore` | Construction history tree + command stack |
    10	| `selectionStore` | Selected and highlighted elements |
    11	| `toolStore` | Active tool and tool family management |
    12	| `viewStore` | Viewport state (zoom, pan) and 3D options |
    13	| `settingsStore` | User preferences (grid, snap, styles) |
    14	| `authStore` | Google authentication state |
    15	
    16	## processStore
    17	
    18	Manages the `ProcessTree` and `CommandStack`:
    19	
    20	**State:**
    21	- `root: Process` — the process tree
    22	- `commandHistory: CommandStack` — undo/redo stacks
    23	
    24	**Getters:**
    25	- `getNodeByPath(path)` — find node by tree path
    26	- `visibleSubprocesses` — subprocesses respecting encapsulation
    27	- `allActions` — flat list of all actions
    28	
    29	**Actions:**
    30	- `addSubprocess()`, `addActionGroup()`, `addAction()`
    31	- `removeSubprocess()`, `removeActionGroup()`
    32	- `clear()` — reset everything
    33	
    34	## selectionStore
    35	
    36	Tracks which elements are selected and highlighted:
    37	
    38	**State:**
    39	- `selectedIds: Set<string>` — currently selected element IDs
    40	- `cascadeHighlightIds: Set<string>` — dependents highlighted on hover
    41	
    42	**Computed:**
    43	- `hasSelection` — boolean, any selection exists
    44	- `selectionCount` — number of selected items
    45	
    46	**Actions:**
    47	- `selectSingle(id)` — replace selection with one element
    48	- `addToSelection(id)` — add to current selection
    49	- `toggleSelection(id)` — add or remove (Shift+click)
    50	- `clearSelection()` — deselect everything
    51	- `selectMultiple(ids)` — replace with a set
    52	- `setCascadeHighlight(ids)` — highlight dependents on hover
    53	
    54	## toolStore
    55	
    56	Manages the active construction tool:
    57	
    58	**Tool families** (toolbar groups):
    59	- Point family: Free Point, Intersection
    60	- Line family: Line, Parallel, Perpendicular, Tangent variants
    61	- Segment family: Segment
    62	- Circle family: Circle
    63	- Guide family: Guide
    64	- 3D Elements: 3D Point, 3D Line, 3D Segment, 3D Plane, Aux. Projection Plane
    65	- Examples: Folding Plane Setup, Quick 3D Line
    66	
    67	**Features:**
    68	- Last-used tool per family (persisted in `localStorage`)
    69	- Keyboard shortcuts: `P`, `L`, `S`, `C`, `G`, `I`, `Esc`
    70	- Tool activation/deactivation lifecycle
    71	- Argument collection state for multi-pick tools
    72	
    73	## viewStore
    74	
    75	Controls the 2D viewport and 3D display options:
    76	
    77	**State:**
    78	- `zoom`, `panX`, `panY` — 2D canvas viewport
    79	- `gridVisible` — grid toggle
    80	- `threeViewOptions` — 3D view toggles (persisted in `localStorage`)
    81	- `showProjectedElements` — show 3D projections on planes
    82	
    83	**Actions:**
    84	- `setZoom(level)`, `setPan(x, y)`
    85	- `adjustZoom(delta, focusPoint)` — zoom toward cursor
    86	- `resetViewport()` — return to default view
    87	
    88	## settingsStore
    89	
    90	User preferences, auto-saved to `localStorage`:
    91	
    92	**Categories:**
    93	
    94	| Category | Settings |
    95	|----------|----------|
    96	| Grid | Visibility, spacing, snap-to-grid |
    97	| Snap | Enabled, snap radius |
    98	| Default Styles | Colors and line widths for points, lines, circles, guides |
    99	| 3D View | Default visibility of planes, projection lines |
   100	
   101	All settings use deep watchers for automatic persistence.
   102	
   103	## authStore
   104	
   105	Manages Google OAuth2 authentication state for Drive integration.

===== docs/api/services.md =====
     1	# Services API
     2	
     3	Reference for Diedrico's service layer.
     4	
     5	## PaperService
     6	
     7	Converts domain geometry into drawing primitives for SVG rendering.
     8	
     9	```typescript
    10	class PaperService {
    11	  generatePrimitives(context: PrimitiveContext): Primitive[]
    12	  generate3DProjectionPrimitives(context: PrimitiveContext): Primitive[]
    13	}
    14	```
    15	
    16	**Pipeline**: Iterates all planes → iterates all elements → resolves visibility → generates primitives with resolved styles.
    17	
    18	**Primitive generators**: `pointToPrimitive()`, `lineToPrimitive()`, `segmentToPrimitive()`, `circleToPrimitive()`, `halfHingeLineToPrimitive()`, `guideToPrimitive()`.
    19	
    20	**Style resolvers**: `resolvePointStyle()`, `resolveLineStyle()` — apply hover, selected, cascade-highlight, snap, and active-plane states.
    21	
    22	## SVGRenderer
    23	
    24	Converts paper primitives to SVG attributes.
    25	
    26	```typescript
    27	class SVGRenderer {
    28	  transformPoint(point: Vector2, viewport: Viewport): Vector2
    29	  inverseTransformPoint(screenPoint: Vector2, viewport: Viewport): Vector2
    30	  renderPoint(primitive: PointPrimitive): SVGAttributes
    31	  renderLine(primitive: LinePrimitive, viewport: Viewport): SVGAttributes
    32	  renderCircle(primitive: CirclePrimitive): SVGAttributes
    33	  renderPath(primitive: PathPrimitive): SVGAttributes
    34	}
    35	```
    36	
    37	**Viewport transform**: `(paperCoord + pan) × zoom → screenCoord`
    38	
    39	## Element2DRenderer3D
    40	
    41	Renders 2D elements in the Three.js 3D scene.
    42	
    43	```typescript
    44	class Element2DRenderer3D {
    45	  renderElements(world: World, options: RenderOptions): THREE.Group
    46	  renderPlaneElements(plane: ProjectionPlane, world: World): THREE.Group
    47	}
    48	```
    49	
    50	**Transforms**: Uses `applyMatrix4x3()` with plane's `planeTo3D` matrix. Points → spheres, Lines → tubes, Circles → tube rings.
    51	
    52	## ProjectService
    53	
    54	Handles persistence via command-sourcing.
    55	
    56	```typescript
    57	class ProjectService {
    58	  serialize(world: World, commandStack: CommandStack): ProjectData
    59	  deserializeAndReplay(data: ProjectData, world: World, commandStack: CommandStack): void
    60	}
    61	```
    62	
    63	**Schema migration**: Automatically upgrades older project files (snake_case → camelCase, flat → hierarchical IDs).
    64	
    65	## GoogleDriveService
    66	
    67	Browser-to-Drive integration via `gapi.client`.
    68	
    69	```typescript
    70	class GoogleDriveService {
    71	  initialize(): Promise<void>
    72	  signIn(): Promise<void>
    73	  signOut(): Promise<void>
    74	  signInSilently(): Promise<boolean>
    75	  fetchUserProfile(): Promise<UserProfile>
    76	  listFiles(): Promise<DriveFile[]>
    77	  getRecentFiles(): Promise<DriveFile[]>
    78	  createFile(name: string, content: string): Promise<string>
    79	  updateFile(fileId: string, content: string): Promise<void>
    80	  readFile(fileId: string): Promise<string>
    81	  deleteFile(fileId: string): Promise<void>
    82	}
    83	```
    84	
    85	**Scope**: `drive.file` (app-created files only) + `userinfo.profile` + `userinfo.email`.
    86	
    87	## DependencyService
    88	
    89	Manages bidirectional element dependency tracking.
    90	
    91	```typescript
    92	class DependencyService {
    93	  registerNode(outputId: string, inputIds: string[]): void
    94	  unregisterNode(elementId: string): void
    95	  getDependents(elementId: string): string[]
    96	  getDependencies(elementId: string): string[]
    97	  getAllDependents(elementId: string): string[]  // transitive
    98	  getAllDependencies(elementId: string): string[]  // transitive
    99	}
   100	```
   101	
   102	## ValidationService
   103	
   104	Validates geometric feasibility and user inputs before operation execution.
   105	
   106	## InputService
   107	
   108	Handles canvas mouse/touch events, hit-testing, and tool argument routing.
   109	
   110	## RenderService
   111	
   112	Low-level rendering functions (used primarily in tests): `renderPoint()`, `renderPlane()`, `renderLine()`.

===== docs/contributing/testing.md =====
     1	# Testing
     2	
     3	Diedrico uses a comprehensive testing strategy with Vitest for unit/integration tests and Playwright for E2E tests.
     4	
     5	## Test Runner
     6	
     7	### Unit & Integration Tests
     8	
     9	```bash
    10	cd frontend
    11	npm run test           # Run once
    12	npm run test:watch     # Watch mode
    13	npm run test:coverage  # With coverage report
    14	```
    15	
    16	Uses **Vitest** with `jsdom` environment.
    17	
    18	### E2E Tests
    19	
    20	```bash
    21	cd frontend
    22	npx playwright test
    23	```
    24	
    25	Uses **Playwright** with Chromium. Configuration in `playwright.config.ts`.
    26	
    27	## Test Organization
    28	
    29	```
    30	frontend/tests/
    31	├── unit/
    32	│   ├── domain/           # Domain model tests
    33	│   │   ├── math/         # Vector/matrix operations
    34	│   │   ├── world/        # World, planes, elements
    35	│   │   └── processTree/  # Process tree operations
    36	│   ├── commands/         # Command and operation tests
    37	│   ├── services/         # Service layer tests
    38	│   ├── tools/            # Tool behavior tests
    39	│   └── utils/            # Utility function tests
    40	├── integration/
    41	│   └── ...               # Cross-layer integration tests
    42	└── e2e/
    43	    └── ...               # Full browser tests
    44	```
    45	
    46	## Writing Tests
    47	
    48	### Unit Test Example
    49	
    50	```typescript
    51	import { describe, it, expect } from 'vitest'
    52	import { FreePoint2D } from '@/domain/world/FreePoint2D'
    53	
    54	describe('FreePoint2D', () => {
    55	  it('should realize at the specified coordinates', () => {
    56	    const point = new FreePoint2D('p1', 'projPlane1', 3.5, 2.0)
    57	    const world = createTestWorld()
    58	    const realization = point.realize2D(world)
    59	
    60	    expect(realization.feasible).toBe(true)
    61	    expect(realization.position[0]).toBeCloseTo(3.5)
    62	    expect(realization.position[1]).toBeCloseTo(2.0)
    63	  })
    64	})
    65	```
    66	
    67	### Test Helpers
    68	
    69	Common patterns used in tests:
    70	
    71	- `createTestWorld()` — creates a minimal `World` with base planes
    72	- Direct operation calls — `op.apply(world)` to set up state
    73	- Realization checks — validate geometry via `realize2D()` / `realize3D()`
    74	- Feasibility checks — verify `realization.feasible` for valid/invalid inputs
    75	
    76	## Test Categories
    77	
    78	### Domain Tests
    79	
    80	Test pure geometric logic: element creation, realization computation, dependency tracking, coordinate transformations.
    81	
    82	### Command Tests
    83	
    84	Test operation apply/undo/redo: verify world state after each step, ensure perfect reversal on undo.
    85	
    86	### Service Tests
    87	
    88	Test PaperService primitive generation, SVGRenderer transforms, ProjectService serialization/deserialization.
    89	
    90	### Tool Tests
    91	
    92	Test tool argument collection, multi-pick flows, keyboard shortcuts, tool switching.
    93	
    94	### Integration Tests
    95	
    96	Test complete workflows: create elements → modify → undo → verify.
    97	
    98	### E2E Tests
    99	
   100	Test the full UI: canvas clicks, panel interactions, selection behavior, keyboard shortcuts.
   101	
   102	## Test Baseline
   103	
   104	The project maintains a known baseline of pre-existing test results. When running the full test suite, compare against this baseline to detect regressions.
   105	
   106	## Tips
   107	
   108	- Run tests frequently during development
   109	- New features should include tests covering:
   110	  - Happy path (element creation, realization)
   111	  - Edge cases (degenerate inputs, infeasibility)
   112	  - Undo/redo correctness
   113	  - Serialization round-trip
   114	- Use `toBeCloseTo()` for floating-point comparisons
   115	- Test feasibility propagation when adding dependent elements

===== docs/architecture/overview.md =====
     1	# Architecture Overview
     2	
     3	Diedrico follows a layered architecture with clear separation between domain logic, state management, commands, and UI.
     4	
     5	## Layer Diagram
     6	
     7	```
     8	┌─────────────────────────────────────────────────┐
     9	│                  Components (Vue)                │
    10	│  CanvasPanel · ThreeView · ElementsPanel · ...   │
    11	├─────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
    12	│                 Stores (Pinia)                   │
    13	│  toolStore · selectionStore · viewStore · ...    │
    14	├─────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
    15	│               Commands & Operations              │
    16	│  CommandStack · Command · IOperation impls       │
    17	├─────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
    18	│                   Services                       │
    19	│  PaperService · SVGRenderer · ProjectService     │
    20	├─────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
    21	│                Domain Model                      │
    22	│  World · ProjectionPlane · Element2D/3D          │
    23	└─────────────────────────────────────────────────┘
    24	```
    25	
    26	## Data Flow
    27	
    28	### User Action → Domain Change
    29	
    30	```
    31	User clicks canvas
    32	  → InputService detects click target
    33	  → Active Tool receives argument (point, line, etc.)
    34	  → Tool creates Command with Operations
    35	  → Operations mutate World (add element, update position)
    36	  → World.markDirty() increments version
    37	  → Observers re-render (2D canvas, 3D view, panels)
    38	```
    39	
    40	### Rendering Pipeline
    41	
    42	```
    43	Domain (definitions)
    44	  → realize2D() computes intrinsic (u, v) coordinates
    45	  → planeToPaper2D matrix transforms to paper space
    46	  → PaperService generates drawing primitives
    47	  → SVGRenderer converts to SVG elements with viewport transform
    48	```
    49	
    50	## Key Design Decisions
    51	
    52	### Definition vs Realization
    53	
    54	Elements store **what they are** (definition), not **where they are** (coordinates). Coordinates are computed lazily via `realize()` and cached. This enables:
    55	
    56	- Clean undo/redo (only definitions change)
    57	- Efficient recomputation (only dirty elements re-realize)
    58	- Consistent serialization (save definitions, not coordinates)
    59	
    60	### Command Sourcing
    61	
    62	Persistence saves the command sequence, not world state. On load, commands replay to reconstruct everything. This ensures perfect fidelity and enables schema migration.
    63	
    64	### Plane Ownership
    65	
    66	Each 2D element belongs to exactly one projection plane. Elements use hierarchical IDs (`{planeId}/{localId}`) to encode this relationship. This avoids cross-plane ambiguity and simplifies lookup.
    67	
    68	## Technology Stack
    69	
    70	| Layer | Technology |
    71	|-------|-----------|
    72	| Framework | Vue 3 (Composition API) |
    73	| State | Pinia stores |
    74	| 2D Rendering | D3.js + SVG |
    75	| 3D Rendering | Three.js + CSS2DRenderer |
    76	| Math Labels | KaTeX |
    77	| Build | Vite |
    78	| Testing | Vitest + Playwright |
    79	| Backend | Express (minimal REST API) |

===== docs/guide/persistence.md =====
     1	# Persistence & Google Drive
     2	
     3	Diedrico uses **command-sourcing** for persistence: projects are saved as a sequence of commands that can be replayed to reconstruct the state.
     4	
     5	## Project File Format
     6	
     7	Projects are stored as `.diedrico` JSON files:
     8	
     9	```json
    10	{
    11	  "id": "project-uuid",
    12	  "name": "My Construction",
    13	  "schemaVersion": "6.0.0",
    14	  "created": "2024-01-01T00:00:00.000Z",
    15	  "modified": "2024-01-15T12:00:00.000Z",
    16	  "nextElementId": 42,
    17	  "commands": [
    18	    {
    19	      "operations": [
    20	        {
    21	          "type": "AddFreePoint2DOp",
    22	          "parameters": { "planeId": "projPlane1", "u": 3.5, "v": 2.0 },
    23	          "outputs": { "pointId": "projPlane1/point2d5" }
    24	        }
    25	      ]
    26	    }
    27	  ]
    28	}
    29	```
    30	
    31	### Why Command-Sourcing?
    32	
    33	- **Full replay**: The exact sequence of user actions is preserved
    34	- **Undo/redo**: Native, since the command stack is the save format
    35	- **Migration**: Schema upgrades can transform operation parameters
    36	- **Compact**: Only stores deltas, not the full geometric state
    37	
    38	## Google Drive Integration
    39	
    40	Diedrico integrates directly with Google Drive from the browser — no backend required.
    41	
    42	### How It Works
    43	
    44	1. **OAuth2 authentication** via Google Identity Services
    45	2. **`drive.file` scope** — the app can only access files it created
    46	3. Files stored in a `diedrico/` folder in the user's Drive
    47	4. Session persistence via `localStorage`
    48	
    49	### Operations
    50	
    51	| Action | Description |
    52	|--------|-------------|
    53	| Sign In | OAuth2 popup, grants `drive.file` + profile scopes |
    54	| Save | Serializes current `World` + `CommandStack` → uploads JSON |
    55	| Load | Downloads JSON → replays all commands to reconstruct state |
    56	| List | Shows recent `.diedrico` files from the app folder |
    57	| Delete | Removes a project file from Drive |
    58	
    59	### Configuration
    60	
    61	Google Drive requires a Google Cloud project with the Drive API enabled. See the [Google Drive Setup Guide](/GOOGLE_DRIVE_SETUP) for configuration details.
    62	
    63	## ProjectService
    64	
    65	The `ProjectService` handles serialization and deserialization:
    66	
    67	- `serialize(world, commandStack)` → `ProjectData` JSON
    68	- `deserializeAndReplay(data, world, commandStack)` → reconstructs state
    69	- Automatic schema migration from older versions (snake_case → camelCase IDs, hierarchical IDs, etc.)
    70	
    71	## Schema Versioning
    72	
    73	| Version | Changes |
    74	|---------|---------|
    75	| 6.0.0 | Current — camelCase IDs, hierarchical element IDs |
    76	| Earlier | Various migrations applied automatically on load |

===== docs/guide/tools.md =====
     1	# Construction Tools
     2	
     3	Diedrico provides 21 construction tools organized into 8 families. Select a tool family from the toolbar, then choose a specific tool from the dropdown.
     4	
     5	## Selection Tool
     6	
     7	**Shortcut**: `Esc`
     8	
     9	The default tool. Click elements to select them, Shift+click for multi-select, click empty space to deselect.
    10	
    11	## Point Family
    12	
    13	### Free Point (`P`)
    14	
    15	Click on the canvas to create a point at that position on the active projection plane.
    16	
    17	### Intersection Point (`I`)
    18	
    19	Click two intersecting elements (lines, circles, segments) to create a point at their intersection. For line-circle and circle-circle intersections, two intersection points are created (indexed 0 and 1).
    20	
    21	## Line Family
    22	
    23	### Line Through Two Points (`L`)
    24	
    25	Click two points to create a line passing through both, extending infinitely.
    26	
    27	### Parallel Line
    28	
    29	Click a reference line, then click a point. Creates a line through the point, parallel to the reference line.
    30	
    31	### Perpendicular Line
    32	
    33	Click a reference line, then click a point. Creates a line through the point, perpendicular to the reference line.
    34	
    35	### Tangent to Circle Through Point
    36	
    37	Click a circle, then click a point outside the circle. Creates a tangent line from the point to the circle.
    38	
    39	### Tangent to Circle Parallel to Line
    40	
    41	Click a circle, then click a reference line. Creates a tangent line to the circle that is parallel to the reference line.
    42	
    43	## Segment Family
    44	
    45	### Segment (`S`)
    46	
    47	Click two points to create a finite segment between them. Segments behave like lines for intersection purposes but have defined endpoints.
    48	
    49	## Circle Family
    50	
    51	### Circle (`C`)
    52	
    53	Click a center point, then click a radius point. Creates a circle with the center at the first point and radius equal to the distance to the second point.
    54	
    55	## Guide Family
    56	
    57	### Guide (`G`)
    58	
    59	Click a hinge line (or broken hinge line), then click a through-point. Creates a guide line perpendicular to the hinge, used for transporting distances between projection planes. Sibling guides are auto-created on the paired plane.
    60	
    61	## 3D Elements Family
    62	
    63	### 3D Point
    64	
    65	Click two corresponding points on different projection planes. Reconstructs the 3D point from its 2D projections.
    66	
    67	### 3D Line
    68	
    69	Click two corresponding lines on different projection planes. Reconstructs the 3D line from its 2D projections.
    70	
    71	### 3D Segment
    72	
    73	Click two corresponding segments on different projection planes. Reconstructs the 3D segment, automatically resolving endpoint pairing.
    74	
    75	### 3D Plane
    76	
    77	Click three non-collinear 3D points. Defines a 3D plane through all three.
    78	
    79	### Auxiliary Projection Plane
    80	
    81	Click a trace line on an existing projection plane to create a new auxiliary projection plane folded along that trace.
    82	
    83	## Examples Family
    84	
    85	### Folding Plane Setup
    86	
    87	Creates a standard setup with P_H, P_V, and the hinge line traces. Useful as a starting point for new constructions.
    88	
    89	### Quick 3D Line
    90	
    91	Creates a complete 3D line example from two predefined 2D line projections with a single click. Useful for testing the 3D view.
    92	
    93	## Tool Behavior
    94	
    95	- **Stay active**: Most tools remain active after creating an element, letting you create another immediately
    96	- **Return to selection**: Example tools auto-return to the selection tool after executing
    97	- **Keyboard shortcuts**: Single-key shortcuts (P, L, S, C, G, I, Esc) for quick tool switching
    98	- **Argument collection**: Tools that require multiple picks show visual feedback for the current state

===== specs/001-dihedral-learning-mvp/data-model.md =====
     1	# Data Model: Diedrico MVP
     2	
     3	**Date**: 2025-12-22 (Updated: 2026-01-06)  
     4	**Purpose**: Define domain entities, relationships, validation rules, and state transitions
     5	
     6	---
     7	
     8	---
     9	
    10	## Phase 98: Dynamic Action Descriptions
    11	
    12	### Overview
    13	
    14	Process Tree action descriptions are computed dynamically from current element state (labels and construction parameters) rather than stored as static strings. This ensures descriptions always reflect current element names even after renaming.
    15	
    16	### Functional Requirements
    17	
    18	**FR-065**: Process Tree action descriptions MUST be computed dynamically from current element state (labels, construction parameters) rather than stored as static strings. When elements are renamed or dependencies change, action descriptions MUST automatically update to reflect current state. Description format MUST follow pattern: "{ElementType} {CurrentLabel}: {ConstructionMethod}" where construction method includes current parent labels (e.g., "Point A_5: Intersection of line L_2 and circle C_1"). This ensures Process Tree always displays accurate, up-to-date construction history even after elements are renamed.
    19	
    20	---
    21	
    22	## Phase 99: Clickable Labels in Process Tree
    23	
    24	### Overview
    25	
    26	Element labels in Process Tree action descriptions become interactive - clicking a label selects the corresponding element in the application. This leverages the existing DescriptionPart infrastructure from Phase 98 which already includes `elementId` for all label parts.
    27	
    28	### Architecture
    29	
    30	**No Domain Changes Required**: This is purely a presentation layer enhancement. The descriptor system from Phase 98 already provides all necessary data (DescriptionPart with elementId).
    31	
    32	### Component Changes
    33	
    34	#### ProcessTreeNode.vue
    35	
    36	**Template Enhancement** (Lines ~150-152):
    37	
    38	```vue
    39	<!-- BEFORE (Phase 98) -->
    40	<template v-for="(part, index) in descriptionParts" :key="index">
    41	  <span v-if="part.type === 'text'">{{ part.content }}</span>
    42	  <span v-else v-html="formatElementLabel(part.content)"></span>
    43	</template>
    44	
    45	<!-- AFTER (Phase 99) -->
    46	<template v-for="(part, index) in descriptionParts" :key="index">
    47	  <span v-if="part.type === 'text'">{{ part.content }}</span>
    48	  <span 
    49	    v-else 
    50	    class="label-clickable"
    51	    :data-element-id="part.elementId"
    52	    @click.stop="handleLabelClick(part.elementId, $event)"
    53	    v-html="formatElementLabel(part.content)"
    54	  ></span>
    55	</template>
    56	```
    57	
    58	**Script Enhancement**:
    59	
    60	```typescript
    61	import { useSelectionStore } from '@/stores/selectionStore';
    62	
    63	const selectionStore = useSelectionStore();
    64	
    65	function handleLabelClick(elementId: string | undefined, event: MouseEvent) {
    66	  if (!elementId) return;
    67	  
    68	  event.stopPropagation(); // Prevent action node selection
    69	  
    70	  if (event.shiftKey) {
    71	    // Multi-select: add to selection
    72	    selectionStore.addToSelection(elementId);
    73	  } else {
    74	    // Single select: replace selection
    75	    selectionStore.selectSingle(elementId);
    76	  }
    77	}
    78	```
    79	
    80	**CSS Enhancement**:
    81	
    82	```css
    83	.label-clickable {
    84	  cursor: pointer;
    85	  transition: all 0.15s ease;
    86	  border-radius: 2px;
    87	  padding: 0 2px;
    88	}
    89	
    90	.label-clickable:hover {
    91	  background-color: rgba(100, 150, 255, 0.1);
    92	  text-decoration: underline dotted;
    93	}
    94	
    95	.label-clickable:active {
    96	  transform: scale(0.98);
    97	}
    98	
    99	/* Ensure KaTeX content inside clickable labels inherits pointer cursor */
   100	.label-clickable .katex {
   101	  cursor: inherit;
   102	}
   103	```
   104	
   105	### Selection Flow
   106	
   107	**User Action → System Response**:
   108	
   109	1. **User clicks label** "A_5" in Process Tree action description
   110	2. **handleLabelClick** fires with elementId="point2d5"
   111	3. **selectionStore.selectSingle** called (or addToSelection if Shift)
   112	4. **Selection state updates** (selectedIds contains "point2d5")
   113	5. **Reactive updates triggered**:
   114	   - Elements Panel: Scrolls to A_5, shows selected state
   115	   - SvgCanvas: Highlights point A_5 with selection color
   116	   - ThreeView: Highlights point A_5 (if visible in 3D)
   117	   - ProcessTree: Shows action that created A_5 as selected, related actions highlighted
   118	
   119	### Edge Cases
   120	
   121	**Deleted Elements**:
   122	- DescriptionParts for deleted elements return empty array (Phase 98 behavior)
   123	- No labels rendered → no click handlers → no issue
   124	
   125	**Plane Elements**:
   126	- Projection plane labels (P_H, P_V, auxiliary) are clickable
   127	- Clicking plane label selects the plane (shows in Elements Panel, no 2D highlight as planes don't have point representation)
   128	
   129	**Multi-Plane Selection**:
   130	- User can select elements from different planes simultaneously (Shift+click)
   131	- All selected elements highlighted on their respective planes
   132	- 3D view shows all selected 3D elements or 2D element projections
   133	
   134	### Testing Strategy
   135	
   136	**Unit Tests**: (Not applicable - purely template/interaction)
   137	
   138	**Integration Tests**: ProcessTreeNode.spec.ts
   139	- Test label click selects element
   140	- Test Shift+click adds to selection
   141	- Test click event doesn't propagate to action node
   142	- Test plain text spans not clickable
   143	
   144	**E2E Tests**: processTree.spec.ts
   145	- Create point A_1, line L_1 through A_1 and A_2
   146	- Click "A_1" label in line action description
   147	- Verify A_1 becomes selected in Elements Panel
   148	- Verify A_1 highlighted in canvas
   149	- Shift+click "L_1" label in another action
   150	- Verify both A_1 and L_1 selected
   151	
   152	### Performance Considerations
   153	
   154	**Click Handler Overhead**: Negligible - one event listener per action node, delegates to labels via event.target
   155	
   156	**Selection Update Performance**: Already optimized in Phase 98 - selectionStore updates trigger single reactive update across all components
   157	
   158	**Hover Effects**: Pure CSS - no JavaScript overhead
   159	
   160	### Implementation Complexity
   161	
   162	**Estimated Effort**: 2-4 hours
   163	- Template changes: 5 minutes (3 lines)
   164	- Script logic: 15 minutes (handleLabelClick function)
   165	- CSS styling: 15 minutes (hover effects)
   166	- Testing: 2-3 hours (integration + E2E)
   167	
   168	**Risk Assessment**: **Low**
   169	- Leverages existing Phase 98 infrastructure (elementId already present)
   170	- No domain model changes
   171	- No descriptor changes
   172	- Pure presentation enhancement
   173	- Well-defined selection API (selectionStore)
   174	
   175	### Future Enhancements (Not Phase 99)
   176	
   177	**Ctrl+Click Toggle Selection**: Add toggle mode where Ctrl+click toggles element in/out of selection
   178	
   179	**Context Menu on Label**: Right-click label shows context menu with "Select", "Highlight Dependencies", "Jump to Element", "Properties"
   180	
   181	**Drag-and-Drop from Label**: Drag label to canvas to create reference or constraint
   182	
   183	**Label Tooltips**: Show element details on hover (type, plane, coordinates, feasibility)
   184	
   185	---
   186	
   187	### Architecture
   188	
   189	#### Action Interface Changes
   190	
   191	**Current Action Interface** (Phase 97):
   192	```typescript
   193	export interface Action {
   194	  elementId: string;        // Element created by this action
   195	  description: string;      // STATIC description stored as string
   196	  isOutput: boolean;        // Phase 87: Output flag
   197	}
   198	```
   199	
   200	**New Action Interface** (Phase 98):
   201	```typescript
   202	export interface Action {
   203	  elementId: string;              // Element created by this action
   204	  operationType: string;          // Operation type (e.g., "AddFreePoint2D", "AddLineLineIntersection")
   205	  constructionParams: ConstructionParams;  // Structured construction metadata
   206	  isOutput: boolean;              // Phase 87: Output flag
   207	}
   208	
   209	// Construction parameters vary by operation type
   210	export type ConstructionParams = 
   211	  | { type: 'free' }
   212	  | { type: 'lineLineIntersection'; line1Id: string; line2Id: string; intersectionIndex: number }
   213	  | { type: 'lineCircleIntersection'; lineId: string; circleId: string; intersectionIndex: number }
   214	  | { type: 'circleCircleIntersection'; circle1Id: string; circle2Id: string; intersectionIndex: number }
   215	  | { type: 'lineThroughPoints'; point1Id: string; point2Id: string }
   216	  | { type: 'lineParallel'; lineId: string; pointId: string }
   217	  | { type: 'linePerpendicular'; lineId: string; pointId: string }
   218	  | { type: 'lineTangent'; circleId: string; pointId: string }
   219	  | { type: 'segment'; point1Id: string; point2Id: string }
   220	  | { type: 'circle'; centerId: string; radiusPointId: string }

===== specs/001-dihedral-learning-mvp/for-planing.md =====
     1	# Not yet planned
     2	
     3	This file contains diverse ideas which need to be included in the project.
     4	
     5	In general, any idea needs to...
     6	
     7	1. Be described in specs.md (unless it is a purely technical issue).
     8	2. Be described in ui-specs.md if it has a UI component.
     9	3. Have its implementation detailed in data-model.md.
    10	4. Be broken down into smaller implementable tasks in tasks.md.
    11	5. Include corresponding testing tasks.
    12	6. Be marked here as done and move it to the already planned section.
    13	7. Implement the new tasks. All of them.
    14	8. Run all the tests. Fix regressions (some tests may need to be changed, fully rewritten or just removed).
    15	9. Commit with git.
    16	
    17	Note that completing these tasks may require asking the user for additional information and clarification. When in doubt, just ask!!!
    18	
    19	
    20	## FOR PLANING
    21	
    22	* [x] Add a Quick Segment tool for creating a sample 3d segment in the same way we have the add quick 3d line.
    23	
    24	* [ ] Add a mechanism for setting drawing properties correctly. The properties may merge properties set at the process tree (and its parent elements), from the projection plane, from the type of object, on the element itself. When representing and geometric object, all this properties must be merges using a priority system. Final settings must follow a gated behaviour (set-value and value or get-it-from-parents). Then we must also have multipliers for things like transparency or width, where a property is calculated as the final setting which higgeer priority in the hierarchy and the combination of all the multipliers.
    25	
    26	* [ ] In many occasions overlaying objects are created (or instance, the (B)HHL over the traces of an auxiliary projection plane). When that happens, some objects my hide or made others difficult to understand (again, for instance, dashed HHL get hidden by the regular lines used to define the projection). In order to overcome that a new entity called the OverlapingGroup must be added. It must be added to all the Element2D objects, so that objects which are created to overlap (complimentary guides, HHL, etc. ) are all added into this entity which must be linked from all the instances representing it. When drawing, its contents must be sorted according to some rules and only the **visible** element on top must be drawn. The sorting rules are that: the elements in the active plane get precedence, guides and (B)HHLs have precedence over regular lines. Note that it must be found which operations introduce elements related into that way to previously existing elements. Also, new Ops must be added to lookupOrCreateOverlappingGroup, mergeOverlappingGroups, addElementToOverllappingGroup, etc. Analyze this in deep. All those ops must also support undoing and redoing (as all the ops do)!
    27	
    28	* [ ] Write the user documentation.
    29	* [ ] Rationalize class names for projection planes and related object (Ops, for instances).Ok
    30	* [ ] Draw the grids in the 3d view, grid granularity must be automatically adjusted as for the 2d view.
    31	* [ ] 2d elements should be represented in the same way in the 3d view as in the main view (for instance, color, dashed lines, etc.)
    32	* [x] Show contextual tool feedback: display what the user has already picked and what is expected next. For instance, "Creating line: point A picked, another point expected"
    33	* [ ] In 3D rotation axis seems to be hardcoded or be limited. For instance, I am not able to rotate the view around the viewer-O axis
    34	* [ ] Add support for moving semi-free points and distances along guide lines.
    35	* [x] Improve element naming: currently points are assigned generic numeric names; implement meaningful default names.
    36	* [ ] Improve how elements are shown in the left world listing widget.
